	<item>
		<title>Abu Dhabi rediscovered through new app</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=841</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 16 Mar 2013 15:25:01 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Zoe Hu]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/derive</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<i>Photo via deriveapp.com</i>

There is always an excuse – it’s too hot, I have work to do, and the classic, there is nowhere to go. These are lines that we sling around like catchphrases as we sit, safe and comfortable, in the confines of Sama Tower, unwilling to venture past the glossy metal gates of our lobby and into the heat and smog of the city.

We make up excuses for why we don’t leave Sama Tower as the city lurks in our bedroom windows. But where does this unwillingness to explore stem from? In a university so familiar with the itch of wanderlust, it is strange that a common complaint is that we rarely leave our dormitories.

The absence of a walking culture in Abu Dhabi is a major issue among the student body. In professor Pascal Menoret’s class, Politics and the City, students met with app developers Eduardo Cachuco and Babak Fakhamzadeh to create a virtual walking guide for the city during a weekend workshop.

The guide, which comes as a downloadable app for smartphones, is called the <a href="//deriveapp.com/s/v2/">Dérive app</a>. The name stems from the Situationist movement in France during the 1950s and 1960s, when people fought to combat commercialism and commodification by encouraging more thoughtful relations to objects and surroundings.

The idea of Dérive is not to provide set destinations. It is something different from the little virtual red pushpins that we rely on when we we use Google Maps to trace our path from one point to another. In fact, the app is the enemy of the little red pushpin – its aim is not to provide routes, but rather to offer tasks or instigators that help refresh one’s perception in the city.

These tasks come in <a href="//deriveapp.com/s/v2/abu-dhabi-2013">cards</a> that you can shuffle with just a tap of an iPhone screen, and they range from requests like turn at the next red light, to follow an alley to its end to something as simple as find silence. The Dérive app, which already has versions designed for cities such as New York, consists of a general set of cards as well as cards that are specifically geared towards one city. It may just be what NYUAD students need to brave the sometimes confusing layout of Abu Dhabi.

The unique features of Abu Dhabi’s urban landscape presented a challenge for members of the creative workshop. The abundance of superblocks and the lanes of traffic that double as pedestrian obstacle courses are two factors that students cite as major struggles when getting to know the city.

“The city is a city that is ostensibly built for cars,” says Charlotte Wang, a participant in the workshop and a junior at NYUAD. “Most of its major pathways are built for cars, so as a walker you’re limited.”

The lack of pedestrian-friendly paths leads to students developing quick, easy routes to where they need to go, routes that they rarely stray from.

“The problem is, we tend to be constrained by the grid and go from Point A to Point B by using big avenues and boring sidewalks," Menoret said. "If we learned to get lost, it would actually make the city more interesting, more livable, more walk-able. [Dérive is] a very cool app that helps you randomize your experience of the city.”

On the first day of the workshop, co-organized with professor Dale Hudson, the students of Menoret’s class went out into the city with the app’s set of general tasks and took notes on their different experiences. The following day, they used these observations to create a special set for Abu Dhabi. The cards, which are also translated into Arabic, have illustrations that were designed by students and faculty of Abu Dhabi.

“What the Dérive app forces you to notice is these everyday things that we overlook,” Wang said. “For example, we had a couple of directives like follow a pedestrian. I’d never noticed how much people walk all over the place. It’s almost chaotic because there’s no set pattern that people are following. I hadn’t noticed that until we were asked to follow someone within a superblock.”

Jennifer Acker, a creative writing professor at NYUAD who is interested in the organization of cities, also attended the workshop.

“I enjoyed thinking about how these cards were or were not pushing me in directions other than what I would have come up with on my own,” Acker said. “I wouldn’t say that the Dérive app changed my impression of the city, but it did show me a way to create something playful and fun and to pay attention to new things.”

The app user can upload pictures of their journey to the Dérive website, as well as offer feedback for future cards. The Dérive combines social media with urban exploration in order to abolish the set, comfortable paths in a city that people often rely on – such as the short walks to DTC and Al Safa – and create something new.

Freshman Nushkia Chamba, who also participated in the Dérive tour, said that the app prompted her to be creative and imaginative in her walking.

“There was no clearly defined path, and we could actually walk between cars, go around them, or go behind a building,” says Chamba. “We had to create our own path.”

<i> Zoe Hu is deputy news editor. Email her at thegazelle.org@gmail.com. </i>]]></content:encoded>
