	<item>
		<title>On the UAE and Public Art</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=54911</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 19 Apr 2014 19:09:11 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Agustina Zegers]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=54911</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_55091" align="alignnone" width="1200"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/12/GLOBAL-GUGENHEIM-RESPONSE-DRAFT.jpg"><img class="size-medium wp-image-55091" alt="GLOBAL-GUGENHEIM-RESPONSE-DRAFT" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/12/GLOBAL-GUGENHEIM-RESPONSE-DRAFT-1200x599.jpg" width="1200" height="599" /></a> <em>Graphic by Emily Wang/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]

&nbsp;
<p dir="ltr">The discussions on the Louvre and Guggenheim Abu Dhabi have so far been extremely convoluted and primarily focused on labor. There have already been <a href="http://hyperallergic.com/117512/protesters-rain-down-thousands-of-bills-in-guggenheim-rotunda/">two protests</a> in the Guggenheim New York this year. Despite agreeing that the circumstances are extremely problematic, it is surprising that so many people jumped the gun to criticize labor conditions only because it is art institutions that are implicated. I sincerely doubt the backlash would have been this strong if we were talking only about UAE labor conditions. Thankfully, the protests and initiatives have brought about big changes in the Guggenheim’s labor standards, but I also sincerely think that many of these outbursts are derived from a much more cynical standpoint. The project was cast aside, portrayed as yet another extravagant construction made to publicize the United Arab Emirates and its development. I want to step away from the discussion of labor and take some time to explore the positive implications of these expansions.</p>
<p dir="ltr">When I first heard about the initiatives for Saadiyat, I quickly joined my friends in sneering at the idea. I was delighted at the prospect of these institutions being available to me, but it all seemed far too artificial and bizarre. I could only see it as a reverberation of the new art market spearheaded by Warhol, Koons and wealthy art consultants. The thought in itself was worthy of a Robert Hughes documentary. However, after living in Abu Dhabi for a year and learning more about the project — I was even present when the director of the Guggenheim was harshly confronted by a journalist at the Talking Art Series — I daresay that my outlook has changed significantly. I may still hate the art market, Warhol and Koons, but I will attempt to make a case for these museum branches.</p>
<p dir="ltr">The way I see it, the reason that this project has caused this much backlash is because a museum is a cultural entity. Creating such a strong, interdependent bond between cultural institutions and economic institutions is understandably dubious. It creates environments that care more about profit than about substance and quality of work. It creates an environment that easily censors art and denigrates the rights of the public in favor of big donations. This is perhaps why everyone has been so quick to criticize these expansion initiatives. The art market is increasingly driven by market demands that are in conflict with what the art world really needs. But it is too simple to think of these museum branches as just another misguided product of this relationship. Although the debate calls important issues to our attention, most of the argumentation surrounding the issue has been entirely disconnected from the purpose of this project: creating quality public spaces for art.</p>
<p dir="ltr">During the Guggenheim Talking Art Series at the Abu Dhabi Art fair last year, Monika Sosnowska discussed her large-scale sculptures and their relation to the war-torn Warsaw. She also showed one of the works that was purchased by the Guggenheim for the region. Large-scale is an understatement: The work filled an entire pavilion from corner to corner. Artists like her stand in the modern spirit of art-making today. This doesn’t mean that all artists make large-scale artworks, but this is something that needs to be considered. With the advent of installation art and large-scale art in the late 1950s, numerous pieces have been relegated to the lifespans of short exhibitions. They only remain in photographs and storage: The artists and art institutions have no space for them, or even their remnants. Museums and art institutions already have thousands of works that go unseen. The British Museum alone has approximately eight million works, and only a tiny percentage of them are shown. Another huge portion of work remains exclusively in private hands for decades. Creating museum branches is an excellent way to extend the lifespan of temporary works and to create public space for the innumerable works in the storages of huge institutions. It is a way to satisfy the demands of a growing number of works, and it provides the means to lengthen the public lifespan of art installations. This viewing public may be small, but that shouldn’t detract from the fact that Abu Dhabi’s investment is providing a space for the public display of important artworks.</p>
The museums being built in Saadiyat are no doubt controversial, but there has been so much debate that we forgot to value the foremost quality of the museum: to make artwork accessible to the public. There is no doubt that there are many issues with respect to the construction of these museums, however the overarching effect of the initiative is undoubtedly positive. The massive structures of the two museums will provide a place for important works that need a space and an audience. It may not do much for the muddied art market, but it is certainly much better than displaying high-art vintage Porsches in fairs — I would recommend reading up on Art Dubai and Abu Dhabi Art. The expansion of the Guggenheim brand is concomitant with the growing art world and an excellent solution to the problem of art surplus. It is also infinitely better than the alternative forms of art display currently available in the country. And although the history of the UAE and its artificial islands didn’t really help the public reception of the concept, there is really nothing better than providing more quality public institutions for art. I would hate to be this optimistic, but the more education and public exposure is given to art, the more the economic underpinning of these projects can subside in favor of their cultural contributions. For now, it may only be a convenient way to import culture into a young country, but ultimately, it is an initiative that will increase art viewership and provide excellent spaces for work and cultural education. I can only hope that the initiatives to improve conditions on the island will bring about real change, and I stand optimistic about the endeavor and its contribution to the cultural landscape of the Gulf and the art world as a whole.

<em>Agustina Zegers is a contributing writer. Email her at opinion@thegazelle.org.</em>]]></content:encoded>
