		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_112051" align="aligncenter" width="764"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2015/04/breakingNews-1200x675.jpg"><img class="size-thumbnail wp-image-112051" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2015/04/breakingNews-1200x675-764x430.jpg" alt="Graphic by Mariko Kuroda/The Gazelle" width="764" height="430" /></a> <em>Graphic by Mariko Kuroda/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]

<i>Editor’s note: The Gazelle has allowed the source in this article to remain anonymous due to the sensitivity of the findings and respect for the students involved. However, the source is willing to speak privately to administration if investigations are undertaken. The Gazelle has been able to verify this evidence with another source, who did not wish to be cited in the article.</i>

New claims have revealed misconduct in voting procedure during the special session General Assembly, of April 22, that voted against the motion to allow students on leave of absence to run in Student Government elections.

During the <a href="http://www.thegazelle.org/issue/64/news/ga-outcome/">special session GA</a>, a group of nine voters had gathered at the Marketplace to watch the livestream on one student’s Google Hangouts account and submit their votes. The students sharing the account voted against the motion by submitting their votes via email. In accordance with Student Government procedure, virtual votes can only be counted if the sender was actively participating on the livestream — meaning they had been watching the video of the GA and paying attention to its procedures.

The group had been sharing the Google Hangout on one computer, and their presence had not been verified on-screen; however, the group all testified to having actively participated by signing a Student Government form after the GA ended.

Student Government released a report that included these signatures. Some members of the community raised doubts over whether the group had all been present in the GA, and whether their votes should be counted. After the Student Government elections, the Executive Board amended voting procedure so that all students livestreaming a GA must use their own Google Hangouts account.

A source has recently come forward saying that the majority of these signatures were forged by a student and member of the voter group. The source was also told, by one of the nine students, that not all the voters in the Marketplace were actively participating on the livestream.

“[The student] told me that not everyone was in the Marketplace watching with [them],” said the source.

The source later spoke again with the student to check these allegations.

“I asked… and [they] said no. I asked specifically, was everyone in the Marketplace, and [they] said no. [They] later said everyone might have not have been there but hanging around,” said the source.

The source also said that two members of the Google Hangout group had personally admitted to them that not all of the signatures on the form were authentic.

“[They] was unable to find everyone or get everyone to sign for themselves so [they] signed on behalf of people. [They] never told me a specific number, [they] told [someone else] that three people signed,” said the source.

The source also added that they had been made aware, through conversations with those involved, that the presidential candidate in the group had known about this misconduct. The presidential candidate had been encouraged to come forward, but had not.

The Gazelle approached the presidential candidate, who chose not to comment. The student signature collector denied the allegations.

<em>Zoe Hu and Joey Bui are editors in chief. Email them at feedback@gzl.me. </em>]]></content:encoded>
