	<item>
		<title>Geo Kamus</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=131</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 18:58:17 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Amanda Randone2]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/geo-kamus</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br />
<p>It may be hard to locate Geo Kamus on a weekend in Abu Dhabi. He could be swimming at an open pool night, skydiving indoors or mastering his Argentine Tango. But Kamus doesn't just welcome new experiences, he engages other students in the process with his passion for connecting students with one another and with opportunities on campus that align with their interests. Kamus won’t simply tell you about the time he went white water rafting. Instead, he will give you the pamphlet, directions and gear to try it out yourself.</p><p>

“Those sorts of informal activities facilitate conversations,” Kamus said. “It’s always interesting to see people’s different perspectives on these cultural activities and I think that in itself is a learning experience in what brings people together.”</p><p>

This nature of connectivity can be attributed to Kamus’ global upbringing, which brought him from the Philippines to Indonesia, and now, to Abu Dhabi.</p><p>

“I can say now an international education gives whole new perspective on things,” Kamus said, describing how the lessons he has learned in academia and culture around the world never remain in one place. He carries these experiences with him and integrates them into any new learning environment.</p><p>

Kamus believes this spirit of bringing people together and inspiring interaction is important for a senator. He sees connecting people, organizations and administration within the university as a way to eliminate the inefficiencies of multiple people trying to accomplish the same goal.</p>]]></content:encoded>
