	<item>
		<title>Angelina Micha Djaja</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=141</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 19:01:49 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Amanda Randone2]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/angelina-micha-djaja</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br />
<p>You may find Angelina Micha Djaja around the NYUAD campus sporting a shirt patterned with elephants, large thick-rimmed glasses and with a viola in tow. The product of an art school in Toronto, Djaja exudes creativity. Her decision to run for treasurer at first seems unusual for someone who loves to read books, perform music and indulge in her artistic side. But this individuality actually aligns with her interest in pursing a position rooted in structure and planning. In addition to having a knack for numbers and organization, Djaja is detail-oriented and enjoys coordinating activities.</p><p>



“Allotting the budget means you will automatically be involved with event planning,” Djaja said. “That is another aspect of the position that I was interested in.”</p><p>



In the past, she has organized benefit concerts and coffee houses to raise money for organizations like Free the Children as the leader of the Social Justice Club at her school. One of her most rewarding experiences involved fundraising for the Artbound initiative, which builds sustainable educational infrastructure in developing countries.
</p><p>


But, Djaja has demonstrated the ability to keep track of money well since before she got involved with event management.</p><p>



“My parents told always told me that I would notice anytime they would take something from me,” Djaja said.  “If someone borrowed money from me, I would never forget."</p><p>



This sharp attention to detail and a commitment to transparency in matters of money and government are what motivated Djaja to run for treasurer. </p>]]></content:encoded>
