	<item>
		<title>Corey Meyer</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=211</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 19:20:34 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Daria Karaulova]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/corey-meyer</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br /><p>A biter, a runner, a leader, a future foreign policy maker and a current sophomore candidate for vice president in the Student Government, Corey Meyer easily combines a well-balanced and confident appearance with an adventurous sparkle in his eyes.</p><p>

Inspired by his classmate’s screams, “It’s just around the corner!” he successfully completed Dead to Red relay in Jordan last year. His dream is to run marathons on each of the continents, including the one along the Great Wall of China.</p><p>

The opportunity that in many ways shaped his NYUAD experience so far is an internship position in the Department of Global Education, last year as UAE and Regional Travel Intern and this year as Chief Ambassador to NYU New York Students. Just recently, Meyer attended a relationship formation meeting between the UAE National Center for Documentation and Research and NYUAD’s Department of Global education, which he believes can potentially result in great opportunities for the NYUAD students to research and to deepen their understanding of UAE history.</p><p>

After serving as president of his high school back home in Columbus, Ohio, running for the vice president at NYUAD seems like a natural decision for Meyer. He observed student government weekly assemblies for the past two years and was a collected member of the Honor Code Committee.</p><p>

Meyer is not just a political science student who admires John F. Kennedy and dreams of working in the U.S. Department of State on policy making and shaping international relations. He is also an active leader of the pre-law, first aid and advocacy student interest groups.</p><p>

For Meyer, life is an unpredictable marathon with challenging hurdles that he is eager to overcome. He said it took him getting thrown out of preschool multiple times for biting his peers, as well as facing a fear of heights, to get this far. Now he is getting ready for his future plans that seem to be seen just around the corner: participation in the Hult Challenge Competition and fulfilling his collection with the marathon in Cyprus.</p>]]></content:encoded>
