	<item>
		<title>Andrew Pitts</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=251</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 19:37:15 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Clare Hennig]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/andrew-pitts</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br /><p>Andrew Pitts, a junior from Philadelphia, is clear about what he hopes to see in the coming year: more integration between the classes. In his bid for presidency of the Student Government, he intends to make it part of his plan to “revolutionize the education.” Events where the students can mingle is not enough, and he wants to see integration on a deeper level and inside the classrooms.</p><p>

“It dismayed me,” Pitts said. “There were no freshmen in my J-Term class.”</p><p>

Of all the classes Pitts is taking this semester, he has only one class with a freshman. In such a small community, it is possible for everyone to know everyone which, as Pitts pointed out, is a blessing and a curse.</p><p>

He compares his experience in NYUAD’s community to his semester abroad at NYU New York, where he struggled to meet people. While he enjoyed spending time with his younger sister, a freshman at the New York campus, he found it difficult to integrate into the community and has since decided to spend the remainder of his undergraduate career in Abu Dhabi.</p><p>

As one of the few students who did not sign the Student Government Constitution when it was first created, Pitts acknowledged his tendency to be critical. He believes that there is always room for dissent and criticism. Ultimately, Pitts believes that Student Government should foster an environment that gets all the students involved.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
