	<item>
		<title>Alex Nyikos</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=271</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 19:41:14 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Zoe Hu]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/alex-nyikos</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br /><p>Alex Nyikos is an anomaly. He lives in modern day society as a busy and socially active university student, yet he does not own a cell phone.</p><p>

“I had a phone for all of three months in my life,” Nyikos admits. “I used it three times, and since lost it and can’t find it.”</p><p>

While most of us see our phone as an additional appendage, Nyikos asserts that the latest iPhone is not necessary to maintain a tight-knit group of friends.</p><p>

“Here, we all live in the same building,” he explained. “It’s easy enough to bump into someone and connect with them. I prefer doing that.”</p><p>

Nyikos’s social circle is a result of not only the serendipity of the Sama run-ins, but also his patience and dedication to relationships.</p><p>

“I like to follow up with people,” he said. “So if I meet them, it’s not like I meet them once and never see them for the rest of the semester.”</p><p>

This type of persistence, difficult to attain as part of university social life, prevails in Nyikos’s hobbies as well – whether it be debate, piano or a game of Halo with friends. A fan of astronomy, Nyikos was in charge of the Observatory at his high school and worked a job at McDonald’s for a summer to earn enough money to buy his own telescope.</p><p>

Nyikos plans to apply the same kind of determination to student government.</p><p>

“I’m kind of tenacious with things,” he said. “If I start doing something, I like to see it through. I don’t like to leave those things half-done.”</p>]]></content:encoded>
