	<item>
		<title>Irene Pañeda</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=291</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 19:44:35 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Nathaniel B. Chumley]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/irene-paneda</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br /><p>Irene Pañeda is as serious about keeping the junior class connected — the majority being abroad — as she is about uniting the diverse peoples and countries of the world. She interweaves the narrative of interconnectedness and understanding by students, citizens and ultimately the human race in her pursuits. She sees and accepts people as citizens of the world.
</p><p>
“The characters are all my friends, they are all imperfect,” Irene said of Anton Chekov’s “Uncle Vanya,” her favorite play.
</p><p>
Pañeda studied abroad in New York City at the Playwrights Horizon Theater School at NYU’s Tisch School of the Arts. She loved the tight, communal feel of the group which reminded her of the NYUAD community.
</p><p>
Pañeda also has a strong passion for interdisciplinary learning. She is a double major in politics and theatre, and she is completing her capstone in political economy. Her capstone concentrates on the reasons for international cooperation through economic causation. Much unlike the characters in “Uncle Vanya,” Pañeda is never preoccupied with imperfection; she believes it is the foundation of international understanding. She is driven and open to the challenges that our generation will face, including her optimistic belief that the current economic crisis will further unite the world community.</p><p>

Pañeda aspires to work with the European Union or another international organization after she graduates. An oasis of wisdom in the desert, Pañeda embodies the idea that, as said by Chekov, “the world perishes not from bandits and fires, but from hatred, hostility, and all these petty squabbles.”
</p>]]></content:encoded>
