	<item>
		<title>Morgante Pell</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=311</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 19:48:08 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Nina Bayatti]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/morgante-pell</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br /><p>Vermont-native Morgante Pell proves that a political degree is not a requirement to demonstrate your passion for student government. As a computer science and economics double major, Pell is chairman of the Research and Development committee. His achievements include developing a school account with Spotify, an online music application that streams music, available in the Campus Lounge.
 </p><p>          As an alternate senator candidate, Pell hopes to continue his work towards identifying areas of improvement within the student body and applying his technical knowledge to alleviate problems.
  </p><p>         “I believe very strongly in humanitarian technology,” Pell said. “Bill Gates is a huge humanitarian. Technology is a way of looking at problems — I would hesitate to use the word technology. I should say, I look at things through models and systems.”
</p><p>           While he is only halfway into his freshman year, Pell is already committed to using his knowledge of technology and social networks to better the campus community. Through creating a simplified version of a social network based on the student population, Pell can see how altering situations can affect student relationships.
</p><p>
“Maybe we can look at how doing Marhaba with upperclassmen here might increase cross-class collaboration,” he proposed.
  </p><p>         As alternate senator, Pell hopes to alleviate the stress involved in running a SIG with the development of an attendance tracker application. He will also advocate for a SIG of the month program to acknowledge the variety of groups operating on campus.
</p><p>           Pell’s commitment to student government will continue no matter the results of the polls.
</p><p>           “I want to make sure we are going in a good direction — staying true to our values as NYUAD, and supporting students and student government,” he said. “Whether I am elected or not, that won’t change. I’ll be going to General Assemblies, and I’ll still be advocating.”</p>]]></content:encoded>
