	<item>
		<title>Clara Correia</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=331</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 19:54:09 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Nina Bayatti]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/clara-correia</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br /><p>
           When 18-year-old Brazil native Clara Correia began her college career at NYUAD she had the intentions of studying political science. But, after taking Nathalie Peutz’s “Anthropology and the Arab World” course, she was inspired to focus on humanitarianism and peace resolutions around the world. </p><p>
           Last semester Correia helped organize “Peace One Day” to commemorate the United Nation’s International Day of Peace on September 21. Additionally, she is working to create a Peace and Conflict SIG with freshman Louis Plottel.</p><p>
           “Part of the purpose is to create this platform where we can foster cultural understanding and enhance knowledge about the conflicts that are going on around the world,” Correia explained.</p><p>
    Correia is making sure that the Peace and Conflict SIG has the necessary foundations needed to sustain its development for the ongoing years by contacting professors and faculty members who could generate greater interest for a successful network.</p><p>
           As an alternate senator candidate, Correia would like to focus her efforts on creating a sustainable leadership transferral system for SIGs.</p><p>

“I realized because of the nature of the university where students go away, especially with the juniors who started a lot of the SIGs, there are some issues with transferring positions and ensuring that the SIG continues,” she said.</p><p>
           The NYUAD freshman would also like to improve communication and transparency among SIG leaders and Student Government. In her past experience working on her student government at her former school in Hong Kong, she was part of a movement to create YouTube videos to inform students of the latest news and decisions to increase transparency.</p><p>
           Ultimately, Correia would like to strengthen the SIGs before Saadiyat, to ensure the establishment and continuity of the program.</p>]]></content:encoded>
