	<item>
		<title>Angela Ortega Pastor</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=371</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 09 Feb 2013 20:02:31 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Alistair Blacklock]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/angela-ortega-pastor</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<br /><p>Despite being only in her second semester at NYUAD, Angela Ortega Pastor can say with conviction that her field of study is economics. The current treasurer for the economics and finance SIG, Pastor thrives on sharing her passion for economics with her friends beyond academics.</p><p>

“It provides a different way of looking at economics,” she said.</p><p>

As treasurer, Pastor has worked on developing budgets with the student government for the past six months. Her goal is to take everything she has learned at the SIG level and to apply herself to the student government, which she believes is vital for nurturing student life.</p><p>

“It’s a really great intermediary between the management and the student body and its, if it does a good job it will be able to represent well what the students want this university to be,” she said.</p><p>

Pastor draws on the political and economic troubles in her home country of Spain to reflect on the importance of a well-run and well-structured government.</p><p>

“Politicians shouldn’t just be politicians, but experts on things like economics,” she said. “Everyone has the opportunity to explore and to expand on their own interests and I think there should be a fair distribution, but we also need to be rational and realistic.”</p><p>

When she is not busy with the economics and finance SIG, Pastor spends her free time exploring Abu Dhabi by foot, whether to find Afghan bread and chai tea or to watch the sunset from the Corniche with her roommates. And whenever her home team, Real Madrid, has a big match, Pastor will certainly be watching.</p>]]></content:encoded>
