	<item>
		<title>Obstacles for transition Student Government demonstrate promise for new team </title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=741</link>
		<pubDate>Mon, 11 Mar 2013 01:51:07 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Julia Saubier]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/obstacles-for-transition-student-government-demonstrate-promise-for-new-team</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>At the crux of NYUAD is a force that thrusts an integral part of university life forward. The newly elected student government is tasked with establishing the pillars that will materialize as traditions, formal systems and regulations. This second-batch of student leaders, faced with a daunting task, is not devoid of challenges but promises to be optimistic and productive. </p><p>

Former vice president Andres Fernandez understands the difficulties rife in Student Government. </p><p>

“You can’t please everyone,” he said. </p><p>

President Leah Reynolds is cognizant of this issue that persists in even the most homogenous student bodies. </p><p>

“It’s always difficult to represent and serve the entire student body well,” she said. “Especially when every individual and class has different interests and concerns.” </p><p>

Because NYUAD is a particularly eclectic institution, the members of student government are trying to adapt to all the different needs of the students. Reynolds said that she and her team want to address current issues and take into account that the student needs and concerns will change as the university grows. </p><p>

“We also want to create an infrastructure that sets up the next student government well, since their term will include both our first graduation and the move to Saadiyat,” Reynolds said.  </p><p>

This transition period for the new student government has not been easy, considering the challenges that the transition to the new campuses poses.</p><p>

As Reynolds explained, the move to Saadiyat is “far enough in the future that we don't know a lot of details about it,” she said. “But, we need to start planning ahead to make sure that whatever infrastructures we build are helpful for that transition.”</p><p>

The transition to Saadiyat will open a lot of new factors including a widened student population, more student resources and an overall different scope of student life.</p><p>

Despite the challenges that lay ahead, the Student Government is working on the fundamentals of creating a strong team.</p><p>

“We all don't know each other well yet personally, and I strongly believe that cohesive student leadership requires both a healthy personal and professional relationship,” said senator Geo Kamus. </p><p>

The upcoming Student Government retreat is an opportunity for the group to bond and learn about each other’s strengths to develop a cohesive vision as team. In a larger context, the university is in a perpetual transitional stage in preparation for the move to the new campus on Sadiyaat Island. </p><p>

“On the bright side, we know the student body we're serving very well, and there is a general openness to innovation and new initiatives,” Reynolds said. </p><p>

Despite these challenges and limitations, the student government’s strength and optimism continues to propel them forward. </p><p>

“Considering our meetings together thus far, I can easily say that we all work well together,” Kamus said. “We are making strides in accomplishing the concerns students have expressed during the Candidate Forum. Each of us has a strong character: our energy translates into productive discussions that are centered towards and sensitive to the needs of the school.” </p><p>

Former president Brett Bolton, a junior at NYUAD, shared his own thoughts of the newly elected staff.</p><p>

"I believe that we have elected a strong team that have handled a bumpy transition incredibly well,” Bolton said. “I know that they have set their eyes on sustainability and they will solidify the institutions and systems that the previous government and our team created while adding their own touches."</p><p>

The success of the first General Assembly is a precursor of all the success to come. Kamus said that he was incredibly happy with the first GA. </p><p>

Reynolds added that she was “happy that the Executive Board and Blacksmith could provide the refreshments—it's cheaper than catering and a great model of how groups can collaborate with each other on campus.”</p>

<br /> <p><i> Julia Saubier is deputy features editor. Email her at thegazelle.org@gmail.com. </i></p>]]></content:encoded>
