	<item>
		<title>NYU Shanghai: Expanding the Global Network University</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=911</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 16 Mar 2013 16:01:11 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Joey Bui]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/shanghai</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><i>Photo courtesy of Casey Kwon</i></p>

<br />

<p>Come September, NYU Shanghai will welcome its first class of 300 undergraduate students.
</p><p>
Since receiving approval from China's Ministry of Education in October, NYU Shanghai has been working closely with members of the NYUAD community as it prepares to join NYU's network as its third portal campus.
</p><p>
NYU Shanghai, the first American university to award degrees in China, shares important traits with NYUAD, said university spokesman Josh Taylor.
</p><p>
"NYU Shanghai will uphold the same academic standards and the same commitment to our teaching and research missions," Taylor said. "It will create an exceptional community comprised of scholars and students whom share a global perspective."
</p><p>
Taylor, who has been working with NYU Shanghai, said NYU Shanghai will be a liberal arts and sciences research university and will provide students with the opportunity to spend up to three semesters abroad in other NYU global campuses.
</p><p>
However, there are a number of factors that differentiate the two. NYU Shanghai has an agreement with China's Ministry of Education, under which at least 50 percent of NYU Shanghai's student population will be Chinese citizens.
</p><p>
NYUAD is not mandated to accept a certain percentage of Emirati students, and the student
population is diverse. In fact, NYUAD Class of 2016 consists of students from 65 different countries.
</p><p>
Henry Bradley, an NYUAD sophomore currently studying in Shanghai and helping with the NYU Shanghai Candidate Weekend, also pointed to this difference.
</p><p>
The NYU Shanghai Candidate Weekend is modeled after that of NYUAD in order to find students who are not only academically excellent but are also the right fit for the school’s environment. The Shanghai Candidate Weekends allow admissions to look for students who can “translate their academic excellence into all facets of life,” according to NYU Shanghai’s <a href="http://shanghai.nyu.edu/newsfeed/entry/nyu-a-view-into-admissions-philosophies-for-us-universities-and-its-in">website</a>.
</p><p>
"Right off the bat obviously you can see a huge difference is going to be when 50% of the student population will be Chinese, so the diversity will not be as sheer as in AD," Bradley said.
</p><p>
Although at least 50 percent of students at NYU Shanghai will be Chinese citizens, all classes will be taught in English. According to the most recent press release for NYU Shanghai, international students will take Chinese language classes with the aim of becoming fluent in Chinese by graduation.
</p><p>
Bradley said he sees the Chinese language requirement as a facilitator for mutual learning.
</p><p>
"It is optimal to have a 50/50 split with a fluent English speaker and a Chinese student living together, which is the plan for room assignments," he added.
</p><p>
NYUAD sophomore Mastish Taddesse participated in the planning process of NYU Shanghai last summer. Taddesse, who was shown the proposed curriculum and plans for NYU Shanghai, said she observed the contrasts between the academic focus of NYU Shanghai and that of NYUAD.
</p><p>
She found that the academic focus of NYU Shanghai would be science and engineering, with less attention to social sciences and humanities. According to the press release, the university will still offer three academic major options: STEM  — Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics — Social Sciences and Humanities.
</p><p>
"Students would not choose NYU Shanghai for the same reason that they choose
NYU Abu Dhabi," Taddesse said. "The academic fields are narrower, but it will draw in students
interested in those specific fields, and especially students interested in working in China, or with
China, in the future."
</p><p>
Bradley said this may be because it has been difficult to convince East China Normal University and the Chinese government that a liberal arts education is valuable and competitive, as China follows the traditional education structure that focuses on degrees streamlined toward a particular career.
</p><p>
He added that the campus is aiming to be on par with NYUAD in terms of academic excellence: "I don't doubt that top students will be sourced," Bradley said.
</p><p>
Bradley said that unlike NYUAD, NYU Shanghai will not be able to provide financial aid as comprehensively as NYUAD because the Chinese government will not support the school financially to the extent that the UAE government is supporting NYUAD, and lower financial aid may limit the pool of talent.
</p><p>
NYU Shanghai will be jointly managed by NYU and East China Normal University, as the first university in China run jointly by a foreign school. The approval by the China's Ministry of Education included the grant of independent legal status for NYU Shanghai.
</p><p>
But the institution may also be facing concerns about the Chinese government's position on academic freedom.
</p><p>
"Criticism [of NYU Shanghai] will stem from restrictions set by the Chinese government," Taddesse said.
</p><p>
Despite NYUAD’s <a href="https://nyuad.nyu.edu/about/faqs.html">policies</a> granting students “full academic freedom as it exists at NYU New York,” the institution has also faced objections relating to this topic because of recent incidents in the UAE.
</p><p>
In February, Kristian Coates Ulrichsen, research fellow at the London School of Economics, was denied entry to the United Arab Emirates because of his political views on Bahrain. In a <a href="http://mideast.foreignpolicy.com/posts/2013/02/25/academic_freedom_and_uae_funding">column</a> for Foreign Policy, Ulrichsen argued that the UAE's threshold for legitimate criticism and academic freedom has lowered significantly in the past year.
</p><p>
In his piece, Ulrichsen also refers to a June 2012 article in the Chronicle of Higher Education titled "<a href="http://chronicle.com/article/A-Careful-Guest-in-a-Foreign/132075/">NYU-Abu Dhabi Behaves Like Careful Guest in Foreign Land</a>," which expressed the concerns of NYU faculty in New York about the issue of academic freedom in the UAE.
</p><p>
But some, including NYUAD professor of literature Deborah Williams, say the presence of NYU in states such as the UAE and China may encourage more academic freedom and global dialogue.
</p><p>
In her article titled "<a href="http://www.thedailybeast.com/articles/2013/03/14/as-nyu-s-president-faces-a-no-confidence-vote-lessons-from-overseas.html?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+thedailybeast%2Farticles+(The+Daily+Beast+-+Latest+Articles)">As NYU’s President Faces a No-Confidence Vote, Lessons From Overseas</a>" for The Daily Beast, Williams wrote that she values the different perspectives of students from around the world that come to the UAE, as they allow the NYUAD community to "live international relations."

<br /> <p><i> Joey Bui is a staff writer. Email her at thegazelle.org@gmail.com. </i></p>]]></content:encoded>
