	<item>
		<title>NYUAD students celebrate Passover with professor </title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=1221</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 06 Apr 2013 18:04:05 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Zoe Hu]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/nyuad-students-celebrate-passover-with-professor</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<img class="mainimage" alt="" src="http://i6.cims.nyu.edu/~adb387/Gazelle/04_07/sederfinal.jpg" />

<i>Photo by Nicole Lopez del Carril/The Gazelle</i>

A satisfying crunch ripped through the air as everyone around the table bit into their matza bread and maror sandwiches. A traditional spread made out of horseradish, the maror was tinged with an alarming magenta color that hinted at its strong, bitter flavor. Around the table, winces and cringes were apparent as Passover Seder participants got their first taste of the dish.

This past spring break, ten NYUAD students had been invited to NYUAD Chemistry Professor Joel Bernstein’s apartment in Tel Aviv, where he and his wife Tzipi Eynot were hosting a Passover Seder dinner — an annual dinner in Judaism that <a href="http://www.bbc.co.uk/schools/religion/judaism/passover.shtml">celebrates</a> the freedom of the Jewish people from Egyptian slavery during biblical times.

For many of the students attending, it was their first brush with Passover, which was apparent in their clumsy matza creations, fumblings with different traditional spreads and awkward, giggly mispronunciations of certain Hebrew words.

“I've celebrated Passover dinners before, although in a more Christian context,” said freshman Geo Kamus, who attended the dinner. “This one focused on Judaism where the tradition originated, so it was very valuable for me to experience it with a family.”

Despite this inexperience, Bernstein and his wife were encouraging and expressed how excited they were to welcome the group. Bernstein patiently explained how to offset the sharp tang of the maror with a different spread, the charoset, which was an autumny paste made of fruits, nuts and apples.

Bernstein said the separate dishes at Passover each had a significant meaning behind them. The bitter maror was supposed to be reminiscent of the plight of the Jewish slaves, and the matza — a flat, crispy bread — is traditionally served at Passover because the Jewish people had to flee Egypt before they could wait for their baking bread to rise. The stories behind each dish were told through an educational dialogue between the head of the table and the children of Bernstein’s family, the youngest of whom was a smiley, baby girl.

The dinner was catered around reading the Haggadah, a book compiled of different prayers, songs and religious acts that guide families through their Seder dinner. The Haggadah encourages Jewish families to invite beggars and travellers — including poor college students — into their homes to share in the different rituals as well as the delicious food.

"Including ‘wayward’ guests at a Sabbath or festival meal has a long history in Jewish tradition,” Bernstein said. “This year our family Passover gathering of ten was blessed by ten NYUAD students, which made it a very special and memorable occasion — one that Tzipi and I could have barely imagined three years ago when we joined the NYUAD community. We hope that there will be more shared experiences like this."

“It was even made more special because they are professors here at NYUAD whom I know I will always have a relationship with,” Kamus said.

NYUAD students are often welcomed into the homes of the university’s staff and faculty, where they are able to sit and talk with their professors outside the classroom setting. These privileges include holiday dinners or small get-togethers with other students, such as the recent <a href=" http://web.thegazelle.org/sarkozi">Arabic lunch</a> with Professor Nassar Isleem.

These close experiences with professors, although not unique only to NYUAD, are uncommon among many college campuses, and they help create bonds that stretch across the Global Network University. NYU sophomore Bailey Theado found interactions with professors outside the classroom to be completely different from her experience in New York.

“It was definitely something new for me to meet my professors and their families in the elevator or around Sama,” Theado said.

For the students celebrating Passover with Professor Bernstein, they showed their thankfulness by singing “Dayenu,” a song about gratitude. The catchy rhythm supported a combination of Hebrew and English lyrics, and the children around the table sang shyly as Bernstein’s daughter-in-law bounced her giggling baby to the beat. The NYUAD students were not the most in-tune group, but the songs and rituals allowed for more exploration about Judaism.

“I enjoyed singing in Hebrew the most, although I didn't know a lot of what the words meant,” Kamus said. “What was most important was joining in the spirit of thanksgiving, of great company, and great food."

One of the recurring themes of the night was the importance of enjoying the meal with family and friends. In several of its passages, the Haggadah itself actually mandated that diners recline while drinking and eating so that they may be as comfortable as kings. Some students chuckled as, caught off guard, they had to remember to sit back, relax and enjoy the holiday spirit.

<i> Nicole Lopez del Carril is a contributing writer. Zoe Hu is deputy news editor. Email them at thegazelle.org@gmail.com. </i>]]></content:encoded>
