	<item>
		<title>Masdar City as symbolic, yet unrealistic for sustainability</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=1231</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 06 Apr 2013 18:12:38 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Nathaniel B. Chumley]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://web.thegazelle.org/chumley</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<img class="mainimage" alt="" src="http://i6.cims.nyu.edu/~adb387/Gazelle/04_07/chumley.jpg" />

<i>Photo by Jourdan Enriquez/The Gazelle</i>

Masdar City is the self-proclaimed sustainable energy utopia that is powered almost entirely by <a href="”http://www.masdarcity.ae/en/66/whats-next/masdar-headquarters/&quot;">solar energy</a>. There are unmanned electric cars for transportation, solar-powered desalination plants for drinking water, energy efficient palmwood buildings and an innovative sustainable energy research institute developed in cooperation with MIT, curiously entitled, “The <a href="”http://www.masdar.ac.ae/”">Masdar Institute</a> of Science and Technology.”

The city is also a free economic zone, which means there is no income, import or corporate taxes for individuals and businesses, and capital is also free to move. However, the question beckons, is Masdar City just a theme park of cool and innovative inventions, like the unmanned car, or will Masdar City be an important leader in worldwide sustainable energy? Yes, and no.

Some <a href="”http://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-12-08/masdar-city-a-vision-of-a-greener-future-or-emirati-excess-.html”">critics</a> claim that since the Gulf region has the second greatest amount of sunlight to the Sahara, Masdar City cannot truly be a model for the rest of the world, portraying it as a Disneyesque theme park of the future in the Arabian Desert. Other critics state the since Masdar City relies solely on solar power, this does little to reduce global dependence on oil.

Since Masdar City uses little energy due to efficient transportation and sustainable buildings, recycling of desalinated water — the recycling and desalination is run by solar power — and the sustainability of solar energy, it produces a lot more than it consumes. Saudi Arabia buys a large amount of Masdar City’s energy, and this exchange is a monumental example of what the future can look like. DesertTec, a company comprised of a consortium of 12 European energy companies, started a solar power initiative in the Moroccan segment of the Sahara Desert in 2009. Even small segments of the Saharan Desert can be harnessed to support the entire Middle Eastern region, and possibly the rest of the world. However, the thought reoccurs that solar energy is effectively electrical energy, and this would not lessen the need for oil.

More and more, the feasibility and increasing need to radically change our methods of transportation becomes less radical. As seen in Masdar City, energy from the sun powers vehicles that get you from one place to another. Research on ion-powered batteries has increased the length of electrical charge, further enhancing feasibility in the commercial market. We now see the charge distance in electric batteries increasing by the year, and at the same time charge time decreasing. For example, the Tesla Model- S, unveiled in 2009 and awarded the Motor Trend “Car of the Year” in 2013, has an average charge distance of 426 km, or 265 miles.

Will people really take to the idea of walking up stairs, using unmanned electric vehicles, drinking recycled gray water, and for now, living near a desert? Only the future will tell. However, this radical change to this way of life has an ever-increasing feasibility.

As stated on Masdar’s <a href="”http://www.masdar.ae/en/masdar/detail/abu-dhabi-investing-in-an-evolving-world-energy-market”">website</a>: “Abu Dhabi is demonstrating what a responsible oil producer can do to help create a balance between hydrocarbons and renewable energy.” By creating Masdar City, Abu Dhabi and the UAE realize that the use of hydrocarbons and renewable energy sources in tandem paints a more realistic equilibrium to strive towards. The Emirate of Abu Dhabi plans to replace seven percent of its energy consumption with renewable resources, and Dubai pledges for five percent, by 2020.

This slow transition to renewable energies has been the modus operandi for many nations, however, the largest investment in renewable energies has been in Germany. The growth rate in the past decade has been astounding. Germany is predicted to get a third of its energy from sustainable resources by 2020, most notably solar energy.

How has Germany seen so much growth in green technology and sustainable resources? It is from one distinct policy <a href="”http://www.germanenergyblog.de/?page_id=283”">mechanism</a> called feed-in tariffs. These tariffs basically pay sustainable energy companies whenever they input energy on to the grid, or in other words sell energy, sparking the increase in productivity and amount of green tech and sustainable energy companies. This program, coupled with pro-growth tax incentives, has thrust Germany in front of others on the world’s green energy stage.

On the topic of macro incentives for sustainable energy growth, Green Peace criticizes Masdar City for just that. They state that by creating one large city, you are thereby adding a lot of carbon into the environment, and you are not helping to make cities that already exist more sustainable. However, Masdar City will help to make Abu Dhabi, as well as the countries that are buying power from Masdar City, more sustainable. In addition, the intellectual investment of a research institute will lead to better technology and ways towards greater sustainability.

So then, what does this say about Masdar City and its role in the world? The initiative to create a research institute and a haven for state-of-the-art international sustainable energy companies will have large positive externalities for the emirate of Abu Dhabi, and likely the sustainable energy movement as a whole, especially due to the support of MIT, the government of the UAE, numerous sustainable energy agencies from around the world and the United States. However, Masdar City will prove to be more of a symbol and a platform of ideas to build upon.

In terms of greater use of sustainable resources, macroscopic policy changes have seen the greatest results. Retrofitting buildings to be more efficient and imagining new sustainable ways of life in research universities is important, but what the world needs most is a drastic change in where we get our energy, as we will only increase our energy consumption. Much like the phrase in Disney World’s Tomorrowland, “the future is here today,” and in a lot of ways in Masdar City, it is. Masdar City is no amusement park, but it is a utopia, which can be used as a model but will not lead to the greater changes that we need to see in energy sustainability throughout in the world.

<i> Nathaniel B. Chumley is a contributing writer. Email him at thegazelle.org@gmail.com. </i>]]></content:encoded>
