	<item>
		<title>Through my Eyes: From Moroccan History Classrooms to Algerian Refugee Camps</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=18441</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 19 Oct 2013 17:46:58 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mohamed Amine Belarbi]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=18441</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p dir="ltr">Around every corner is a person who claims to be a peace promoter, an advocate of justice or the reincarnation of Mother Teresa. But what we fail to realise is that words, as strong and influential as they may be, don’t have the impact necessary to bring about true change. Preaching love is one thing, but when it comes down to a decisive choice between your interests and your ideals, it becomes tricky to uphold the latter.</p>
<p dir="ltr">I am from Morocco, and as some may know, my country’s history and geography is subject to controversy. With one national discourse claiming that we freed the nation from the vices of colonialism and recovered the historic Morocco of our forefathers and another asserting that we exploited the departure of the Spaniards in the south to invade the Western Sahara region, acing your history exam becomes somewhat a mission impossible.</p>


[caption id="attachment_17961" align="alignnone" width="600"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_1_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi.jpg"><img class="size-medium wp-image-17961" alt="Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_1_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi-600x337.jpg" width="600" height="337" /></a> <em>Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]
<p dir="ltr">Today, I am far from preaching my national discourse and am more sympathetic to people I was told just yesterday were enemies. Coming to this conclusion was not the result of a passionate presentation I saw in school, but the fruit of a risky adventure that took me to the brinks of death and jail. As stubborn as I am, I decided one day to go to the refugee camps in Algeria where the Saharawi—people who claim Morocco invaded their portion of land and drove them away to the Algerian desert with Napalm and Apache Helicopters — lived. I was the fourth Moroccan to ever visit the camp — the three before me are now either in jail or in exile under charges of high treason and conspiring with the enemy.</p>


[caption id="attachment_17941" align="alignnone" width="600"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_3_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi.jpg"><img class="size-medium wp-image-17941" alt="Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_3_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi-600x337.jpg" width="600" height="337" /></a> <em>Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]

[caption id="attachment_17931" align="alignnone" width="600"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_2_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi.jpg"><img class="size-medium wp-image-17931" alt="Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_2_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi-600x337.jpg" width="600" height="337" /></a> <em>Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]
<p dir="ltr">After receiving years of national propaganda, you would expect the camps to be filled with masked men with AK-47s and thousands of helpless people held against their will in order to draw sympathy to a lost cause. But the reality turned out to be different, although I did still encounter masked men with AK-47s. Talking to people, living with a host family, experiencing their hardships and bonding with new friends was a truly life-changing experience. The ability to put a smile, a tear and a face to the rigid conflict I read about in books made that notion of peace and justice a lot more vivid and impactful in my mind.</p>
<p dir="ltr">In the past, it was a natural response for me to be pro-Moroccan while discussing the Western Sahara issue. At that time the only information I had received, as well as the ultimate version of history taught in school and by the media, had never been challenged by alternative sources, at least not within the Moroccan border.</p>
<p dir="ltr">But as I was brought into a new setting where I was claiming something everybody refuted, things started crumbling inside my head and the pro-Moroccan obvious for me began to change. Is it enough to walk out with a rigid and unshakable opinion just for the sake of claiming that you have been undefeated in a debate? That you have fulfilled your part as a citizen by defending your country’s stance and trashing the secessionists’ claims?</p>
<p dir="ltr">Was I holding onto the truth only because I believed in it, or is the truth a common good to which everybody had access? Perhaps the truth is a common property we are all doomed to partition. Although that approach may not ultimately be the most just and fair, it would at least lay foundations for a consensus on what is true and what is not.</p>
<p dir="ltr">By challenging my own perception of the conflict, I tried to prove to myself that there is only one way to be mentally comfortable when unveiling your convictions, and that’s through building strong bases for your position. These bases should be brought to life by directly witnessing and researching at the place of issue, criticizing ourselves before others do and never taking things for granted.</p>


[caption id="attachment_17951" align="alignnone" width="600"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_4_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi.jpg"><img class="size-medium wp-image-17951" alt="Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/03/morocco_4_Mohamed-Amine-Belarbi-600x337.jpg" width="600" height="337" /></a> <em>Photo by Mohamed Amine Belarbi/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]
<p dir="ltr">Next time you want to change the world, preach peace or bridge the divide between cultures and religions, let your actions speak on your behalf, live the conflict from both sides and reach out to everyone involved in the issue. It takes more than a set of articles or documentaries to relate to those suffering from a conflict. You need to feel the pain they go through before you can offer help and support because through pain and emotional connections true passion for resolution emerges.</p>
<p dir="ltr">If you care about the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, fly to Palestine and live with a family under siege, or spend your next break with an Israeli family to understand why they believe that they have a claim for the land. If you wish to see an end to Sudanese conflict, live their struggle and gain an understanding of what both parties want by reaching out to them. A huge chunk of our actions stems from incomplete understanding of what goes on in the world or are the result of naive ideas that we can put an end to all of the world's evils. Conflicts are far more complex than just two stubborn countries fighting each other: Religious, cultural, economic, racial, political and social factors come together to shape the world as it is. If we can't get a grasp of how these factors come into play by being exposed to them, then we cannot lay a claim to solve conflicts in the first place.</p>
<em>Mohamed Amine Belarbi is a contributing writer. Email him at <em>editorial@thegazelle.org. </em></em>]]></content:encoded>
