	<item>
		<title>Gulf Elite Responding to Sexist Claims</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=23661</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 02 Nov 2013 22:39:58 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mohamed Amine Belarbi]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=23661</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_23921" align="alignnone" width="600"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/04/Belarbi_reply.jpg"><img class="size-medium wp-image-23921" alt="Belarbi_reply" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/04/Belarbi_reply-600x225.jpg" width="600" height="225" /></a> <em>Graphic by Dorothy Lam/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]
<p dir="ltr">Gulf Elite has recently been one of the major topics of discussion amongst students in NYUAD, and while we have been greatly delighted to hear that there is a rising interest in the publication, we thought it necessary to step in and address the issues raised by the article <a href="http://www.thegazelle.org/issue/18/opinion/bergen-3/">Gulf Elite Perpetuates Media Sexism</a>.</p>
<p dir="ltr">Of the 30-plus articles that Gulf Elite has written, the editorial unfortunately only chose two or three to focus on. The sexist label put forth pertains to less than ten percent of our content, or less than seven percent as of today with the release of our <a href="http://gulfelitemag.com/gulf-elite-3/">third issue</a>.</p>
<p dir="ltr">The article claimed that one of Gulf Elite’s writer objectified the Female Economist of the Year 2010 from Sweden Saana Azzam in our first issue when he commented on her clothing and sex appeal. In fact, the writer of the article happened to be her cousin, and the article had been personally reviewed by Azzam herself before publication. The Gazelle’s article neglected to consider that Azzam had a choice in her own portrayal.</p>
<p dir="ltr">Regarding the comment about how the Dress for Success article is only directed toward a male readership, we made it clear in the article that the magazine would soon include an article on female fashion in later issues, and this is is something we are doing at the moment as a female fashion writer has recently joined our team. We come back again to the same point: the article from The Gazelle failed to inquire into all aspects of the subject, and part of that inquiry involves contacting Gulf Elite directly. If the writer had approached us beforehand on the matter of female fashion, I am sure we could have had a nice chat to clarify the matter.</p>
<p dir="ltr">We concede to Bergen, though, that one comment might have come across as sexist. When one writer asked, "Who needs a woman when one can have a Ferrari?" he was expressing his personal opinion and values, and as questionable as they are, they are still his own. As a publication, we do not censor our contributors' thoughts or opinions.</p>
<p dir="ltr">Moreover, Gulf Elite approached the head of NYU Abu Dhabi's Women’s Leadership Network on Oct. 17, asking for female contributors. We explained that Gulf Elite writers were mostly male and that the female point of view was missing from our pieces. We stressed that the subject of women leadership and entrepreneurship was one of our focuses, and given the nature of Women’s Leadership Network and their work, their members were highly qualified to address these topics. However, we never received any response.</p>
<p dir="ltr">To take this goal even further, we are empowering women as a publication, and our content supports this claim. Out of our three Elite profiles, two were women and for the fourth issue, we we are including an Elite profile of a scientist and financier who has been recognized by Forbes as one of the 100 most powerful Arab Women and one of the 500 most powerful women in the world. These facts were surprisingly absent from the editorial.</p>
<p dir="ltr">An interesting fact about Gulf Elite is that, although we started with a male-only pool of writers, we have managed to attract more female contributors who now represent 35% of our writing staff. Our goal at Gulf Elite is to increase female representation to that of male writers in order to convey the views of both genders on topics most relevant to youth today.</p>
<p dir="ltr">When we started Gulf Elite, we made a bet that youth and Generation Y were looking for a publication that differentiates itself from all the conventional pieces they have been accustomed to. We made a bet that youth were genuinely interested in moving forward, creating a new dynamic and learning from each other in order to make it to the top. We made a bet that education as it is now does not offer all that a student needs to thrive in life, that classrooms were not the only places where we could gain knowledge. We made a bet that the future will be determined by entrepreneurs and proactive youth who want to create their own jobs instead of looking for them. Given our expanding readership and positive feedback, we believe we made a winning bet.</p>
<p dir="ltr">It’s been less than three months since we started, and as any venture, we do have several limitations and issues to attend to. To the best of our ability we are trying to improve and enhance our content and reader experience, but given the pace at which we have been expanding, it is hard to keep track of every detail. We believe Gulf Elite will be the next Time or Wired Magazine, and while the journey might be full of challenges and hardships, we promise our readers that we will not spare an effort to make the magazine meet their expectations and even exceed them.</p>
<em>Mohamed Amine Belarbi is a contributing writer. Email him at editorial@thegazelle.org. </em>

<em>Editor's Note: WLN has recently issued a public statement on Facebook, clarifying that they responded to Belarbi's email on 20 Oct, 2013, before this article was published. They had respectfully declined his offer to collaborate.</em>]]></content:encoded>
