	<item>
		<title>NYUAD Relief Effort Sends Funds to Philippines</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=33861</link>
		<pubDate>Sun, 08 Dec 2013 00:47:23 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Joey Bui]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=33861</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="" align="alignnone" width="640"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/09/Filipino_courtesy-of-Kimi-left-align.jpg"><img alt="Filipino_courtesy of Kimi left align" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2013/09/Filipino_courtesy-of-Kimi-left-align.jpg" width="640" height="640" /></a> <em>Courtesy of Kimi Rodriguez</em>[/caption]
<p dir="ltr">The NYU Abu Dhabi community fundraising effort for the Filipino typhoon relief came to a close with a total of 13,820 AED raised and sent to Habitat for Humanity on Tuesday, Nov. 5. Junior Kimi Rodriguez, freshman Moonie Sohn and sophomore Geo Kamus, who all call the Philippines their home, headed the fundraising project.</p>
<p dir="ltr">Rodriguez chose to donate the funds to Habitat for Humanity because of its post-typhoon <a href=" http://www.give2habitat.org/philippines/ReBuildPhilippines">rebuilding program</a>.</p>
<p dir="ltr">"The money goes toward building homes," said Rodriguez. "It's been three or four weeks [after the disaster] ... we thought what people needed most was post-typhoon relief, so cleaning materials, stuff for building, wood."</p>
<p dir="ltr">She also said that the fundraising amount exceeded her expectations. The final milestone set by the team was 15,000 AED.</p>
<p dir="ltr">"One student gave 500 dirhams. It was really very moving; I didn't even know who he was," said Rodriguez.</p>
<p dir="ltr">NYUAD students studying abroad also reached out to the team and asked for advice regarding which organization they should send donations to.</p>
<p dir="ltr">For Sohn, the sense of community created by the NYUAD relief effort was more important than monetary donations. She said that no monetary amount could be enough for the typhoon relief.</p>
<p dir="ltr">"I think the symbolic significance of the school coming together — as much as our conscience allows, or as much as our conscience is intrigued to give — is the real value," said Sohn.</p>
<p dir="ltr">The fundraising effort reached beyond students. Faculty members made up a large part of the donations. On one of the final days of fundraising, the Downtown Campus security staff approached the team with a donation they had collected.</p>
<p dir="ltr">The team set four different milestones for the fundraising at 3,000, 6,000, 10,000 and 15,000 AED. For each milestone reached, Kamus offered prizes: shaving his legs, doing the coconut dance with freshman Patrick Wee, creating a Beyoncé music video and shaving his head in the shape of a raindrop.</p>
<p dir="ltr">“Initially, I didn't think we would reach these numbers, so I was very careless with the prizes we would give out," said Kamus. "[But] it's also something funny that I wanted to do, and this was a good excuse to do it that was publicly condoned."</p>
<p dir="ltr">Sohn said that working on this project made her more optimistic about the NYUAD community.</p>
<p dir="ltr">"I was feeling a little uneasy about NYUAD as a whole ... There was no real point of unity where everyone came together," said Sohn. "But with the relief effort, everyone was united [in] empathy."</p>
<em>Joey Bui is news editor. Email her at editorial@thegazelle.org.</em>]]></content:encoded>
