	<item>
		<title>Mandatory Military Service Bill Proposed by UAE Cabinet</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=36071</link>
		<pubDate>Sun, 26 Jan 2014 00:07:38 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Alistair Blacklock]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=36071</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_37191" align="alignnone" width="800"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/01/United_Arab_Emirates_soldiers_in_U.S._Coast_Guard_training.jpg"><img class="size-full wp-image-37191" alt="100110-N-0803S-001" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/01/United_Arab_Emirates_soldiers_in_U.S._Coast_Guard_training.jpg" width="800" height="693" /></a> U.A.E. soldiers in 2010. Via <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:United_Arab_Emirates_soldiers_in_U.S._Coast_Guard_training.jpg">Wikimedia commons</a>[/caption]
<p dir="ltr">The UAE cabinet announced that military service will be compulsory for all Emirati males between 18 and 30 years old on Jan. 19. The chair of the Cabinet, Vice President and Prime Minister of the UAE and Ruler of Dubai, His Highness Sheikh Mohammed bin Rashid Al Maktoum, tweeted the news to his followers. He said,</p>
<p dir="ltr">
<blockquote lang="en"><p>The new law adds another layer to the national defense force to further protect our nation, secure its borders &amp; preserve its achievements.</p>&mdash; HH Sheikh Mohammed (@HHShkMohd) <a href="https://twitter.com/HHShkMohd/statuses/424853703208603648">January 19, 2014</a></blockquote>
<script async src="//platform.twitter.com/widgets.js" charset="utf-8"></script>
</p>
<p dir="ltr">Ahmed Al Daheri, an NYU Abu Dhabi senior, greeted the news with enthusiasm.</p>
<p dir="ltr">“I think it’s a great opportunity for the citizens of this country to give back to the nation,” he said.</p>
<p dir="ltr">There is currently uncertainty as to when the law will be implemented; however, Al Daheri speculated that it may take one to two years. He hopes that it will be sooner rather than later.</p>
<p dir="ltr">A draft bill has been drawn up to shape a new national defence and reserve force at the request of the President of the UAE, Sheikh Khalifa. It will require all Emirati males who have finished secondary school to undergo nine months of military training, while those who have not completed secondary school will be required to complete two years of training. Service will be optional for Emirati women.</p>
<p dir="ltr">Following the draft law’s recent endorsement by the UAE Cabinet, it will move to the Federal National Council <a href="http://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/uae-plans-mandatory-military-service-males-21588785)">for review</a>.</p>
<p dir="ltr">Different sources have speculated what this new law would mean for Emirati society, with some sources lauding its impact on <a href="http://www.thenational.ae/uae/health/military-service-would-improve-health-of-young-emiratis%20">general health</a>and <a href="http://www.thenational.ae/business/industry-insights/economics/compulsory-military-service-will-help-solve-arab-youth-unemploymen">youth unemployment</a>, as well as bolstering the UAE military reserves. Currently, <a href="http://www.gloria-center.org/meria/1999/03/foley.pdf">reports say that at least some of the lower ranking officers serving the UAE are expatriates</a>.</p>
<p dir="ltr">The new law comes only a few months after Qatar approved a draft law mandating Qatari men to do military service. The legislation, which was produced in November 2013, requires men between ages 18 and 35 to undergo three months of military training, if they are university graduates and four months if they are not.</p>
<p dir="ltr">Kuwait is also debating reintroducing military service, which was <a href="http://news.kuwaittimes.net/conscription-kuwait-investment-future/">suspended in 2001</a>.</p>
<p dir="ltr">As the law is still in development, it is uncertain as to whether Emirati males who undergo military training will have to do so before or after enrolling in university.</p>
<p dir="ltr">But Al Daheri said that nine months of military service could help enhance the UAE’s place in the region.</p>
“A stronger army is just we need to protect our resources, reputation … [it will] send out a stronger message amidst a very unstable region,” he said.

<em>Alistair Blacklock is editor-in-chief.  Email him at editorial@thegazelle.org.</em>]]></content:encoded>
