	<item>
		<title>Faith, Spirituality and College</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=75981</link>
		<pubDate>Sun, 02 Nov 2014 03:29:02 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Janeen Asfour]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=75981</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_77561" align="alignnone" width="1200"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/05/11_2_Opinion_FaithIsrael.jpg"><img class="size-full wp-image-77561" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/05/11_2_Opinion_FaithIsrael.jpg" alt="Graphic by Asyrique Thevendran/The Gazelle" width="1200" height="678" /></a> <em>Graphic by Asyrique Thevendran/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]

For most people, faith and religion are one and the same. There are several reasons why people deem these two words to hold the same meaning or represent the same thing. The first and foremost is the invoking of a divine being in these two words. Some human beings follow an intangible being that is at the center of both words. The second possible reason is that both words seem to draw a large crowd.

It is imperative, however, that we understand the difference between faith and religion and identify which trait belongs to which word. Religion is an institution that was created by people of faith in the past. Whatever religion is or was, people of faith thought of a structure through which they can pursue a mission in line with spiritual beliefs. That was how religion started. It was a way of keeping the faith intact, the believers together, the scriptures unscathed and the traditions — imperative or not — intransient.

However, it did not take long for people to take these institutions of faith and make them instruments of division. As with every other institution, religions had their own leaders, and some ventured into wars in the name of those institutions. The Crusades were just one of many examples, and certainly not the first nor the last. Faith was completely taken out of the equation in those decisions. The only issue on the table, it seems, was the difference between those institutions. Rather than the foundation of the institution, people focused on the structures and traditions of religion, unyoking the aspect of faith from religion. Now we are living at a time when the word “religion” has been marred in sundry ways.

Unfortunately, because people consider both faith and religion to be the same, the former’s reputation has been marred as well. The number of people who have distanced themselves from faith because of the atrocities committed in the name of religion is large. That is why it is important to separate the two.

Faith is not an institution and thus not a human construct. It is a belief in something intangible, and maybe a supernatural being that is beyond our understanding. For most of us, faith is a form of solace during hard times throughout high school, college and careers. The immutability of the object of our faith is what supports us as we move through this very fickle and dynamic world. Beyond that, our faith has been the reason why we, the leaders of the Abu Dhabi Christian Fellowship and Islamic Student Union, are writing this essay together. Our faith is what brings us all together.

Faith, for both of us, has been a bridge between cultures at NYU Abu Dhabi. Faith is not of religion, of culture or of nations. Faith is in and of itself independent, and it is something that most of us here on this campus have in common. It varies in levels and forms but is prevalent nonetheless. This bridge is not just for small talk during awkward religious conferences or Student Interests Groups events. It creates an opportunity for discussion on a deeper level since faith also shapes the personality of each individual. Faith is a way of life.

Faith is a commonality between all humans and is not bound by defined religions. Faith encourages us to serve others. At NYUAD the ISU and the ADCF have weekly meetings to create a community that incorporates service into college life. At our meetings we encourage students to integrate their faith into their journeys in university.

<em>Israel Desta and Janeen Asfour are contributing writers. Email them at opinion@thegazelle.org.</em>]]></content:encoded>
