	<item>
		<title>The Religious Practices of NYUAD’s Staff</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=78791</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 08 Nov 2014 14:17:40 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Allanah Avalon]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=78791</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_79491" align="alignnone" width="1200"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/05/11_9_Features_Profile.jpg"><img class="size-full wp-image-79491" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/05/11_9_Features_Profile.jpg" alt="Photo by Asyrique Thevendran/The Gazelle" width="1200" height="678" /></a> Photo by Asyrique Thevendran/The Gazelle[/caption]

Living among students from all backgrounds and cultures, it is easy to forget that the security and kitchen staff working at NYU Abu Dhabi are expatriates just like us. These staff members represent a range of different countries and religions and, like many students, they face the same struggles inherent in experiencing a new country, a new climate and, for many, new religions. Finding a sense of identity here can be tough, particularly in regard to religion, and many staff members find themselves grappling with new ways to do so in Abu Dhabi.

On the topic of maintaining religious belief in such a diverse community, Richard, a member of the kitchen staff, said that he “find[s] it easy to maintain his religious beliefs and traditions.”

To ensure that this is possible, staff members are given two breaks a day, along with opportunities for prayer breaks when desired, as long as they let their supervisors know in advance.

A fair proportion of security staff are Muslims or come from a Muslim background. Kitchen staff member Nigel agreed that “adjusting to the lifestyle here at NYUAD and in the greater sphere of Abu Dhabi was relatively easy.” He later went onto say that it was “eye opening” to be in a country where religion is such an integral feature of daily life. "[It's] refreshing to see how family- and religion-oriented Emiratis are," he continued.

With Islam the country's official religion, many staff members remarked on a perceived diversity in belief within their work environment.

"[It was] interesting to see such a global range of religions, having never seen such an equality of so many religions in one community [before]," said Roger, a security guard in one of the residential buildings.

Another religion among staff members at NYUAD, as well as in the UAE, is Hinduism.

“I know there is a Hindu community present in Abu Dhabi,” said Simon, a security staff member, “but I have not had the opportunity to truly engage with it, particularly [due to] our hour-and-a-half commute to and from campus everyday.”

Despite conflicts with work commitments, other staff members found the environment in Abu Dhabi to be conducive to maintaining their personal beliefs, no matter what group they identified with.

“It does differ from home,” said Michael, a member of the kitchen team. “But since religion is so present [throughout] Abu Dhabi, it is easy to stay true to yourself and your religion.”

Rachel, a security member, echoed a similar sentiment. “Religion to me is so special,” she said. “Even if I am at home or here being far away from home, no one can take it away from me.”

Jackson, on the security team, expressed that practicing his religion is “relatively similar to home, and easier than I thought it would be.”

<i>Names in this article have been changed to protect the identity of the sources.</i>

<em>Allanah Avalon is a staff writer. Email her at aavalon@thegazelle.org. </em>]]></content:encoded>
