	<item>
		<title>NYUAD Celebrates African and Afro-Caribbean night</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=81501</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 22 Nov 2014 21:23:46 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Allanah Avalon]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=81501</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_82151" align="alignnone" width="1200"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/07/11_22_News_MotoMoto.jpg"><img class="size-full wp-image-82151" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/07/11_22_News_MotoMoto.jpg" alt="Photo by The Gazelle" width="1200" height="678" /></a> Photo by The Gazelle[/caption]

On Nov. 20, the NYU Abu Dhabi community celebrated Moto Moto, the African and Afro-Caribbean night in the East Plaza. The event was organized by the Africa Global Student Interest Group, headed by sophomore Gracious Nyamupachitu and Arame Dieng.

The Office of Student Activities, The Office of Community Relations, The Somali Centre and Paris-Sorbonne University Abu Dhabi were also involved. The Ambassador of Senegal to the UAE was present. Several other African Embassies provided flags.

Moto Moto featured an array of performances that celebrated Afro-Caribbean culture. The night began with a parade from the Campus Center to the East Plaza where students displayed their national flags. Various performances followed: individual dances, songs, poetry, a performance from the Somali Centre, guest drummers from Ghana and a student-choreographed final dance. Additionally, the East Dining Hall served a selection of traditional African and Afro-Caribbean food, such as Jollof Rice, Braai Chicken, Macaroni Pie and Pap.

Nyamupachitu, chairperson of Africa Global, commented on how challenging organising a cultural night can be:

“Hosting cultural events is not an easy task, nor one which is guided with specific rules and pointers. It is a lot of trial and error and brings a series of surprises and setbacks along the way.”

Freshman Rhoshenda Ellis praised the addition of the Afro-Caribbean elements to the event:

“Being a Caribbean and having less than 10 other companions from the Caribbean, I really liked that the African Global Committee included us in their celebrations of Africa. The African diaspora has spread across the entire Caribbean, but I was so happy to hear my music playing through the speakers and to be able to eat food from home.”

<em>Allanah Avalon is a contributing writer. Email her at aavalon@thegazelle.org. </em>]]></content:encoded>
