	<item>
		<title>Mental Health and the Intolerance of Greatness </title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=82991</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 29 Nov 2014 18:57:07 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Oliver Andersson Hugemark]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=82991</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[&nbsp;

[caption id="attachment_83401" align="alignnone" width="1200"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/08/11_30_Opinion_Oliver.jpg"><img class="size-full wp-image-83401" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/08/11_30_Opinion_Oliver.jpg" alt="Illustration by Lucas Olscamp/The Gazelle" width="1200" height="500" /></a> <em>Illustration by Lucas Olscamp/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]
<p style="text-align: center;"><i>“I await myself in the future, where I ‘make an appointment with myself on the other side of that hour, of that day, or of that month.’ Anguish is the fear of not finding myself at that appointment, of no longer even wishing to bring myself there.” — Jean Paul Sartre</i></p>
I wish to ask why at NYU Abu Dhabi we have set the task to become great, rather than to be great for what we already are; for what reason does one try to build castles when one cannot even live in one’s own body?

In the end, is it not a matter of whether captivating narratives of greatness and success are worthy of the price of broken young souls? If the task we have set for ourselves is, as it after all seems, to be shapers of tomorrow, then I am grimly considering staying in bed for an indeterminate future, considering the number of students in this university who are suffering from mental and emotional difficulties.

I did not write this to be explicit, or clear, or anything of the like; I have no intention to tell you where I stand. It should be noted that I am, myself, also torn between a multitude of abstract ideals, torn between the value of what I am and what I have been made to want to become, as I sleep and as I am awake, day in and day out.

After all, why could it not be a matter of balance? Balance, not for a normative appeal, but for the sake of indemonstrable, yet immediately agreeable, reasonableness. What have we become but bigotry inclined megalomaniacs, where the idle artist, low-spirited introvert, the thoughtful innocent and sadly even the conforming student are crushed by lofty ideals that make them seem lackluster, little, not enough.

I thought what made NYUAD unique was the courage to pursue a broader notion of what it means to be successful, to be a new paradigm in higher education where there is more to the story than the story itself. But in light of the rarity of meeting someone with time for a human moment stripped of the stress, the drama, all the student complexes, in light of the far reaching prevalence of heart wrenching anxieties, cries and tensions, my notion of what NYUAD meant is slowly turning against me.

It does not seem to be a matter of our students being sensitive intellectuals, or a matter of the way the real world works. It seems to be a matter of fundamental values, specifically, a matter of what our everyday intuitions will render most important. Some may say that this is not the school for someone with the opinions just outlined, but in response: Is a school where the default setting seems to be more a matter of anxiety and opportunism, than of gratitude and exchange, a school for anyone?

In view of the shortness of life and the fragility of the human mind, our minds, maybe there should be more room than for only conceptuality, life-hostile attachments to probable futures, and fanciful temporary infusions of joys made up of air. There should be room for the heart itself, for its yearnings and sorrows — when one lives in a desert it may be important to save the little water one has got, instead of running towards the horizon looking for an oasis. But you may disagree.

Is it not, as Sartre thought, the fear of not finding ourselves at that appointment in the future, perhaps even the fear that we may not even want to show up, that makes us all, in some sense of the word, a little mentally ill?

&nbsp;

<em>Oliver Andersson Hugemark is a contributing writer. Email him at opinion@thegazelle.org.</em>]]></content:encoded>
