	<item>
		<title>Impressions: Expectations of a Thursday Night </title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=97961</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 28 Feb 2015 21:40:07 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Joey Bui]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=97961</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[&nbsp;

[caption id="attachment_98051" align="aligncenter" width="1200"]<a href=" "><img class="size-full wp-image-98051" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2015/02/justin.png" alt="Graphic by Justin Lee/The Gazelle" width="1200" height="678" /></a> <em>Graphic by Justin Lee/The Gazelle</em>[/caption]

As the sun dips under the haze-shrouded skyline, the sky quickly fades to a majestic purple. Footsteps shuffle as classes end, and the taxi line stretches beyond the usual lot. Breathing a sigh of relief: Thank god it’s Thursday.

Not so fast. From rushing overdue papers to a quick sweat in the gym, there’s business to take care of before the weekend is actually here. Binge-watching Netflix on a Thursday evening then becomes rather an infrequent treat.

Once the plates are wiped clean, what’s next? Periodically scrolling through Facebook Messenger or checking group chats, the expectation of a good night becomes a mere hassle.  Sometimes the choice is obvious: another bizarre event on the Corniche, a friend turning 20 or a desire for Chinese hot pot. Other nights, our expectations looming over us, settling for rom-coms or attempting to crack the weekend’s workload becomes O.K..

Being in a close-knit community that boasts of well-rounded students, it isn’t difficult to become conscious of one’s social life. Watching a nice group race past to catch the bus makes you wonder why you’re the one sitting inside. The regurgitated advice to go beyond your comfort zone, uttered to freshmen constantly from Marhaba Week to First Year Dialogues can be elusive.

“Oftentimes the urge to socialize is out of boredom and it’s Thursday night; sometimes you just need to get away from life,” said freshman Kyler Meehan.

“Being isolated on Saadiyat limits our options on a weekend night, and activities off the island often require lots of money and effort, which means we’re more inclined to stay in,” added freshman Mark Surnin.

The sense of mundaneness on a Thursday night is common. In lieu of the nightly grinds during weekdays, weekend nights often become a cherished opportunity to depart from the academic regimen and simply relax.

Perhaps, for many who have learned to seek pleasure from low-key nights, the urge to socialize on a Thursday night is merely a natural symptom of our developing mental capacities as college students.

For many seniors, such peer pressure isn’t a big deal:

“Being a senior, you are more willing to say no, ‘cause you know what you want, especially if you have an established friend circle,” said senior Nour Al Gharibeh. “[The pressure] is common everywhere and is a natural part of us as social beings.”

“It really depends on your peer group,” echoed senior Mohit Mandal.

“Sometimes it’s just a desire to change an environment, but if I didn’t go out, I would be able to cover it by doing what I wanted to do,” senior Roberto El Khoury said.

So it is O.K. to be the one staying in, saying no and streaming Netflix or finishing a paper. The value you hold to those around you isn’t defined by your presence at every Thursday night occasion, but rather the day-in and day-out practices of care and understanding you offer to them.

The repeated sense of disappointment in any facet of life often moderates our expectations and can push us to try new things. This goes for a Thursday night too: a bowl of 30 dirham pho at <a href="https://www.zomato.com/abudhabi/hanoi-naturally-al-markaziya">Hanoi Naturally</a> or popping in at the monthly open mic at <a href="https://www.facebook.com/arthousespeaks">Art House Speaks.</a> Even easier, try sitting at a different table and get to know that person you so frequently say, hey, how are you?, but never get the chance to learn more about.

At the end of the day, the night will always end and there’ll always be a morning to wake up to — sometimes one that’s more meaningful and worth an earlier bedtime. And once in a while, nights with minimal planning and low expectations — they’ll be ones worth remembering.

Some of my best nights so far haven’t been the most costly or hyped-up, but rather those that keep the conversation going: nights where the walk back to your room leaves you thinking a little deeper, questioning a little further and simply admiring the privilege of still being here for tomorrow.

<em>Justin Lee is a columnist. Email him at feedback@gzl.me</em>]]></content:encoded>
