        'sweden': {
            value: '34',
            latitude: 59.3500,
            longitude: 18.0667,
            tooltip: {content: "Sweden"},
            raptextdict: [{
                songText: "Yung Lean - Yoshi City",
                youtubeUrl: '<iframe width="1280" height="720" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/iX1a3JngmpI?rel=0&amp;showinfo=0" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen></iframe>',
                recommendedBy: "(recommended by sophomore Esh Abdel Rahman)",
                extraText: "&ldquo; Whether or not [Yung Lean] is, strictly speaking, a rapper, is a matter of dispute. A native Swede, he certainly doesn't emanate from the milieu which we most commonly associate with rap, i.e. the American inner city. Indeed, I would claim that the Sadboys are a sub genre of rap as they do not conform to the traditional conventions of this musical aesthetic. Traditional US rap places a heavy emphasis on the lyrics, whilst the melody serves as a peripheral element. This, however, is the reverse in the Sadboys sub genre, where the rap itself plays only a secondary function, complementing the beat. The paraphernalia associated with Sadboys music has great artistic merit, in my opinion, as it is reveals great reflexivity and is subtly satirical of gangster rap. Also, in terms of musical style, Yung Lean draws much of his inspiration from Japanese and Oriental popular culture. The Sadboys movement is a relatively new phenomenon. Indeed, Yung Lean was born in 1996 and is to date only 18 years old. Consequently, his music and associated art is pre-occupied with more millenial themes and is far more globalised in its inspiration and circulation (proportionately) than conventional rap. As suggested by his name, the artist is comparatively young and so we can expect continued development in the years to come,&rdquo wrote Rahman. &ldquo; [In ‘Yoshi City,’] Yung Lean reiterates his loneliness; an unfamiliar theme in conventional rap. There is a sense of friction between Lean's postmodern style and his lyrics, which are focused on the themes of nineteenth century romanticism: isolation and dejection. The rapper is unashamed to reveal previously unknown vectors of emotional sensibility, and this to me is a moment of cathartic proportions in a musical tradition previously marked by a hypermasculinised and aggressive self representation,&rdquo; added Rahman."
                }],
