	<item>
		<title>Autism in the UAE, Part I</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=103791</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 04 Apr 2015 17:32:11 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Siba Siddique]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=103791</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_103311" align="alignnone" width="1200"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/11/04_04_Features_Autism.png"><img class="size-full wp-image-103311" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/11/04_04_Features_Autism.png" alt="Graphic by Mariko Kuroda/TheGazelle" width="1200" height="678" /></a> <em>Graphic by Mariko Kuroda/TheGazelle</em>[/caption]

It is time for recess. All the students have left for the playground except for one girl, who goes to the whiteboard, writes a name with a marker and points to herself. A child on the autism spectrum, this student would often stand up and leave midway through her lessons, despite being one of the most dedicated in the class. Yet the name on the board represented a huge improvement in knowledge association and was a perfect illustration of the brightness of children on the autism spectrum.

Dana AlHosani, a freshman student and volunteer at Future Centre for Special Needs, told this story with pride in her smile, remembering the incident as a fulfilling moment of progress from her time volunteering. FCSN is a centre in the UAE that works with and aims to provide education for people with special needs, including those with autism, a neurological and developmental disorder.

It's hard to diagnose a child with Autism Spectrum Disorder before they are a year old, since the first symptoms are normally noticeable by the age of two or three. The main areas which impact people on the autism spectrum are social interaction, verbal and nonverbal communication and behavioral dysfunction. Early intervention is crucial for treating the effects of autism, and the delay in assessment of and screening for autism leads to misdiagnosis in some cases.

Though there is a statistically high number of incidents of Autism Spectrum Disorder among families living in the UAE, there is no official number available, and awareness of autism in the UAE is hindered by the stigma surrounding the condition. In light of World Autism Awareness Day last week, a newspaper recorded <a href="http://www.khaleejtimes.com/kt-article-display-1.asp?xfile=data/nationhealth/2015/April/nationhealth_April3.xml&amp;section=nationhealth">600 autistic children</a> on a waitlist in Dubai hoping to secure a place in autism centers and start treatment. The recorded global occurrence of the disorder suggests that one in 68 births is affected by autism, and in the UAE it is as high as <a href="http://asoneautism.ae/">1 in 50</a>.

This year, World Autism Awareness Day carries the theme Employment: The Autism Advantage, which deals with the unemployment faced by adults and children on the autism spectrum.

GoalsUAE, an after-school football program, serves as a way for kids on the autism spectrum to go out, make friends and play with their peers. The founder, Khawla Barley from the United States, has a son with a mild form of autism. Barley says that the dearth of opportunities for children on the autism spectrum to participate in after-school activities in the UAE gave rise to the GoalsUAE program. By involving volunteers from schools and universities, the program encourages kids on the autism spectrum to interact with each other and other people and also raises awareness in the community. Team sports such as basketball and football have helped the children interact with their peers, which is one guiding idea behind GoalsUAE.

Social integration is another challenge for kids on the autism spectrum, and it has only recently emerged in the UAE. A lot of parents and volunteers highlighted the lack of services and special programs geared towards their children in contrast with countries in Europe or the United States, where autism is less stigmatized. 

Due to the influx of people from different countries and different cultures, however, it has become easier in the UAE to provide such services on the part of special needs organizations.

As Ghada Massis, a mother of an autistic child, recounted, “[In] other cultures such as [those in] Europe and the States, from a very early age kids are taught that in the community there are kids with special needs. As those kids grow up, they have a good idea of how to deal with and accept them in the community. But here I feel [people] don’t try to understand [children on the autism spectrum] and how to deal with them.”

Teaching at a special needs school follows a few general guidelines. Though each child’s routine is individualized to suit his or her level, the basic techniques of reinforcing certain skills by example is an important tool used by various centers catering to people with special needs.

“We need to treat them as [we would] any student,” AlHosani said.

Read Part II <a href=": http://www.thegazelle.org/issue/63/features/autism-part-ii/">here</a>. 
<em><span style="color: #000000;">Siba Siddique is a staff writer. Email her at </span>feedback@gzl.me.</em>]]></content:encoded>
