	<item>
		<title>On the Verge of Extinction</title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=101251</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 11 Apr 2015 20:24:17 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mario Zapata]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=101251</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_101821" align="alignnone" width="900"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/11/03_15_Opinion_Zapata.jpg"><img class="size-full wp-image-101821" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2014/11/03_15_Opinion_Zapata.jpg" alt="Photo via Wikimedia Commons" width="900" height="509" /></a> <em>Photo via Wikimedia Commons</em>[/caption]

I was only ten the first and, unsurprisingly, only time I encountered an Iberian lynx. My family and I were driving right on the top of the mountains through a very dense maquis thicket on our way to Sierra de Gata, Extremadura, when something I couldn’t recognize crossed the road and stood, completely paralyzed, in front of our car. My father pressed the brakes immediately, pushing all of us forward and making me bite my tongue.

I looked up to find a creature the size of a humongous cat with the appearance of a lion that must have gotten lost from Africa a million years ago, facing our car still stupefied. Its legs were long and gangly, its fur tawny and spotted, and as it breathed frantically its quasi rabbi beard throbbed to the rhythm of its pulse. Within a second, it was gone.

Such a fleeting accident would have quickly fallen into oblivion if I hadn’t, less than two years later, heard on the radio that the last group of Iberian lynxes in Gata had completely vanished, and that only two populations remained in the world, in Doñana and Sierra Morena. This made the Iberian Lynx the first feline to be on the verge of extinction since prehistoric times. I remember it because the radio anchor made me feel especially upset and guilty when she said that humans and our actions were the main cause for its extinction. However, today, I no longer know whether it’s guilt or indifference that I should feel towards the lynx.

The story of the Iberian lynx has been told as a story of human-induced extermination. In my Biology class in Spain, we learned that it was dictator Francisco Franco’s urbanization plan — which essentially colonized the wild territories of my region, Extremadura, through the construction of dams, villages and roads — that caused the extinction and partial loss of a series of species, including the Spanish lynx. The teacher taught us that the human activity in the new settlements, such as La Moheda de Gata or Vegaviana, quickly modified its natural habitat in a series of ways. Due to the characteristics of these construction projects, most of which were carried out on elevated areas and valleys that had to be deforested and cleaned for the works to begin, the feline’s habitat was directly wiped out.

Ms. Fernandez also taught us that the culture of the first settlers is responsible too for great part of the lynx’s extinction. Along with seeing hunting as a harmless pastime, the settlers thought of the Iberian lynx as a threat to both humans, in spite of being normally innocuous to us, and domestic animals <a href="http://www.agenciasinc.es/Noticias/Los-ataques-de-lince-iberico-a-los-animales-de-granja-aumentan">like</a> poultry and sheep, which further encouraged its persecution. Some locals have heard stories of their ancestors gathering with neighbors to collectively massacre wild lynxes, especially after attacks had occurred.

Many also argue that the indirect effects of human activity are a major reason for their population decline, such as the untold rates of CO2 that Franco’s mining and cement projects released, contributing to increased levels of pollution, and, quite possibly, climate change. However, the most modern, prevalent and prominent form of indirect invasion comes in the form of roads, where one can find the lynx’s leading cause of unnatural death: vehicle strikes. In fact, more than <a href="http://elpais.com/elpais/2015/02/16/inenglish/1424097857_034701.html">21</a> lynxes were run over in 2014 — a figure that doesn’t seem so high when I remember the ease with which I almost became by accident a protagonist in their extinction story myself, less than a decade ago.

This is the story of human-induced extinction that Ms. Fernandez taught us in class and that I heard from the radio anchor; a narrative where human and economic development is presented as the murder of the animal. It is, in other words, a familiar story in which humans are fully responsible for the death of the lynx, similar to that told of any other endangered species.

However, this story raises the question: is that actually the case for the Iberian lynx? While some may argue that human actions are the ultimate driving force of most extinction processes, the case of the Iberian lynx is especially interesting because of its peculiarities that have traditionally been overlooked by the conventional narrative. In fact, the Iberian lynx is by nature perhaps one of the most fragile and least adaptable species on the planet, due to its highly specific diet and the tendency of young pups to savagely fight their siblings after birth, frequently to death. Should we then be held fully responsible for their extinction even when there may have been many other causes leading to it? Another question arises: if we can already see that they are extremely fragile and they never really will become any more adaptable, should we bother preserving them at all?

The Iberian lynx has become a species that doesn’t occupy a particular ecological niche anymore. They are at the top of their food chain. Its prey, the European rabbit, is already kept in check by a different mechanism. No species depends on them, and their conservation would cost us much more than it would benefit us, especially considering that there are little chances they will reclaim their original niche. In other words, their extinction would not make a difference at all in the overarching ecosystem, and stopping development to conserve the species is an enormous cost. Why should we then even try to preserve the Iberian lynx at all?

Right when I am about to let my rational side turn down ecological conservation as a response to the issue of extinction, my emotional side cringes at the possibility of never being able to show these animals to my future children. While letting them go seems like the most reasonable solution, I ask myself one more time if there is truly nothing we can do to stop this tale of death; I wonder whether I’m not just about to opt for the easier, less compromising solution. And as I try to decide whether we should completely stop development to avoid the lynx’s death or ignore the lynx and only watch for our economic interests, only one thing becomes clear: the complexity of the case of the Iberian lynx should help us understand that nothing is black or white in terms of conservation ethics and human responsibility.

<em>Mario Zapata is copy chief. Email him at feedback@gzl.me.</em>]]></content:encoded>
