	<item>
		<title>Why We Don&#039;t Date </title>
		<link>http://localhost/?p=116531</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 02 May 2015 21:47:27 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mario Zapata]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://www.thegazelle.org/?p=116531</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[[caption id="attachment_116151" align="alignnone" width="1200"]<a href="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2015/01/05_02_Opinion_Mario.jpg"><img class="size-full wp-image-116151" src="http://thegazelle.s3.amazonaws.com/gazelle/2015/01/05_02_Opinion_Mario.jpg" alt="Illustration by Mariko Kuroda/TheGazelle" width="1200" height="678" /></a> <em>Illustration by Mariko Kuroda/TheGazelle</em>[/caption]

It was with great discontent that Carrie Bradshaw once mused in the now démodé Sex in the City: “In a city like New York with its infinite possibilities, has monogamy become too much to expect?”

Carrie's is a lament that has become all too familiar in many contemporary Western societies, the overture of a chant intoned by people from all — or no —genders and sexual orientations to this day. This is the verse of a confused generation — many of its members benefiting from a liberal education — that has been told to break all the social norms in relationships and love, but hasn't been told how.

It’s not surprising to see that dating culture at US American colleges is fading faster than ever before, as was <a href="http://www.thecrimson.com/article/2015/1/27/harvard-why-we-dont-date/">noticed</a> by university publications like the Harvard Crimson, whose writers confirmed Sarah Jessica Parker’s 2000s-era premonition. At Harvard, one in five undergrads never even tries dating during college, a recurrent theme that the Crimson blames on hook-up culture in the US.

“We’re young, we’re smart. Why waste time building a relationship when we can get what we need — or at least finish stat homework on time — without the untidiness of feeling?”

While we at NYU Abu Dhabi don’t know the exact statistics on student relationships — unless you were enrolled in Paula England’s contentious <a href="http://www.thegazelle.org/issue/45/news/survey/">Gender and Society</a> class — it is relatively easy to assume that, in this particular case, the trend here doesn't contradict any patterns among our counterparts at NYU New York and other universities in the US. However, I am unsure as to whether it's happening for the same reasons.

It would be easy to assume that in a school of high GPAs, global citizenship and leadership summits, we have forgotten to leave time in our Google Calendars to invest in meaningful relationships. Nevertheless, the pursuit of stability in more conventional relationships seems to be more present at NYUAD than at many other universities, contrary to what one would expect at a school known for its liberal values. In fact, one only has to take a few elevator rides at NYUAD to hear just a couple of remarks on this matter.

What is the reason, then, that many of us fail so miserably at establishing meaningful, long-term romantic connections with others in our community?

It is, in my opinion, a fear of loss. Living with a student body that changes every semester and that, in all likelihood, will end up scattered across the globe after graduation makes it hard to imagine a future together. It's almost impossible to see a relationship through beyond a single semester when most of us have its ever-present expiration date hovering over our heads.

Like a daily morning alarm clock, NYUAD students are continuously reminded of the transient nature of their connections, which splits romantic relationships into two possibilities:

A minority grows stronger, and involved parties sometimes even change original plans to stay together. However, I would argue that the expiry date syndrome often discourages the majority of students from engaging in long-term relationships with others in our community, and hinders many nascent relationships from further commitment.

And while there is nothing wrong with this situation, it does show an interesting social trend among a big portion of students at NYUAD; in particular, those who — much like Carrie Bradshaw — simply want a relationship in the most conventional sense.

Those in the Carrie camp see relationships as investment accounts, set to grow and become something else that lasts forever. Because of this notion, far too often we become love-planners, people who see love as a commodified asset that must arrive only at a certain time, because we don't want to receive it before we are completely sure that we can maintain it meticulously.

And while to many, this take on relationships may seem rational or even obvious, such a view on love inevitably denotes certain fears of failure — fears that our life plans might not go the way we conceived them. It is this particular fear, common at NYUAD, that stops us from fully enjoying romantic relationships for the little time we have here.

I have to admit that such a view on relationships very well serves to maintain conventional social structures like marriage and family. However, it is also a view that does little, if anything, to promote the personal growth that comes from the trial-and-error process of romantic relationships that we try so hard to avoid. Not a very nice prospect for the love-planners, it seems. What, then, is my message for all the Carrie Bradshaws out there?

Though this is something I have only recently learned, I believe that, at NYUAD and anywhere else in the world, only those who are able to face the challenges of not knowing what may come next will succeed in the human quest for meaningful connections; only those who know and accept that relationships have ends will be able to enjoy them while they last; and only those who understand love, or however they choose to label it, as something greater than a check in their bucket list will embrace it in its entirety.

So if you are someone who tends to complain in the elevator about the hardships of finding a relationship at NYUAD, I invite you to do one thing: close your love calendar for a while and reevaluate your dating principles. Perhaps it is your own plans and expectations that are making it all too complicated.

<em>Mario Zapata is copy chief. Email him at feedback@gzl.me.</em>]]></content:encoded>
